# The libc sources a sysroot is produced from, pinned.
#
# One record per line, tab separated, in this order:
#
#   libc  version  licence  sha256  url
#
# The licence column is spelled the way rucc-sysroot's manifest format spells it, because it is
# copied into every line of every produced manifest and a second spelling of the same fact is a
# second thing to get wrong.
#
# musl 1.2.5 is the version Zig 0.16 carries, which is recorded in
# spec/cross-compile/01-research-2026.md in the compiler repository. Pinning the same version is
# what makes the comparison between the two mean anything: a difference between our sysroot and
# zig's is then a difference in how it was built rather than in what was built.
#
# glibc 2.44 is here for its abilist files, which are glibc's own record of which symbol it exports
# at which version node and are what the compiler writes its glibc stubs from. `bin/abilist` takes
# those out of this tarball and nothing else, and the stub comparison in the compiler reads them. The
# release is the newest one at the time it was pinned, which is deliberate: an abilist names symbols
# up to the version it came from, the comparison drops everything above whatever the machine's own
# libc has, and a newer description therefore costs nothing and covers every machine an older one
# covers. M9.5 builds a glibc sysroot from this same row, which is why a libc source belongs here
# rather than in a table of its own.
#
# linux 6.19 is there for its uapi headers, which are the system call interface every Linux target
# compiles against and which no libc provides: `bin/kernel-headers` runs `make headers_install` out
# of this tarball and lays the result out as the two directories the compiler searches after the
# libc's two. The release is the one Zig 0.16 carries, which is not a guess either, because its
# bundled linux/version.h says LINUX_VERSION_CODE 398080 and that is 6.19.0. Pinning the same one is
# what makes a diff between our tree and zig's any-linux-any a difference in how it was produced.
# The licence is the kernel's GPL-2.0 with the syscall note, and the note is the whole reason a
# program that is not GPL may use these headers to make a system call.
#
# Nothing here is fetched without a hash and nothing is fetched from a URL that is not on one of
# these lines. `toolchains/record-hash musl 1.2.5 - <url>` downloads once and prints a line whose
# fourth column is the hash, which is the column that belongs here, so nobody types one from memory.

